:::{.callout-note}
## Goal of this chapter
Study numerical methods for the linear equation
$$
\mat A \vect x = \vect b,
$$
where
$\mat A \in \complex^{n \times n}$
and
$b \in \complex^n.$
:::

Two classes of methods:

. . .

- **Direct methods**:

  * LU decomposition (for general invertible matrices);

  * Cholesky decomposition (for symmetric positive definite matrices)

. . .

- **Iterative methods**:

  * Basic iterative methods based on a splitting;

  * Conjugate gradients;

  * And many more: GMRES, BiCGStab, etc.

. . .

Before discussing these methods, we introduce the concept of **conditioning**.

# Conditioning of linear systems {.smaller}

. . .

- Assume that we wish to calculate numerically the solution $(1, -1)^\t$ of
$$
    \mat A \vect x :=
    \begin{pmatrix}
        1 & 1 \\
        1 & 1 - 10^{-12}
    \end{pmatrix}
    \begin{pmatrix}
        x_1 \\
        x_2
    \end{pmatrix} =
    \begin{pmatrix}
        0 \\
        10^{-12}
    \end{pmatrix} =: \vect b.
$$

. . .

- In Julia, we can calculate the solution with the `\` operator


  ::: {#115fb4da .cell execution_count=2}
  ``` {.julia .cell-code code-fold="false" code-line-numbers="false"}
  A = [1 1; 1 (1-1e-12)]
  b = [0; 1e-12]
  x = A\b
  ```

  ::: {.cell-output .cell-output-display execution_count=3}
  ```
  2-element Vector{Float64}:
    1.0000221222095027
   -1.0000221222095027
  ```
  :::
  :::


  Why is the relative error much larger than the *machine epsilon*?

. . .

- Julia solves not $\mat A \vect x = \vect b$ but
$$(\mat A + \Delta \mat A) (\vect x + \Delta \vect x) = (\vect b + \Delta \vect b)$$
where $\Delta \mat A$ and $\Delta \vect b$ are roundoff errors,
and $\Delta \vect x$ is the resulting perturbation of the solution.

[**Question**]{.red}: Can we estimate $\frac{\norm{\Delta \vect x}}{\norm{\vect x}}$ in terms of $\frac{\norm{\Delta \mat A}}{\norm{\mat A}}$
and $\frac{\norm{\Delta \vect b}}{\norm{\vect b}}$?

## Recalls: vector and matrix norms (1/3) {.smaller}

. . .

Given $p \in [1, \infty]$,
the $p$-norm of a vector $\vect x \in \complex^n$ is defined as follows:
$$
    \norm{\vect x}_p :=
    \begin{cases}
        \left( \sum_{i=1}^{n} \abs{x_i}^p \right)^{\frac{1}{p}} & \text{if $p < \infty$}, \\
        \max \Bigl\{ \abs{x_1}, \dotsc, \abs{x_n} \Bigr\} & \text{if $p = \infty$}.
    \end{cases}
$$
In Julia, calculate $\norm{\vect x}_p$ using `norm(x, p)`.

. . .

::: {#8cef7bc5 .cell execution_count=3}
