## Cholesky for banded matrices {.smaller}

- $\mat A$ is a banded matrix i.e. it exists $b\in\N$ such that $a_{ij}=0$ if $|i-j|>b$.

. . .

- It follows that $\mat C$ is also a banded matrix with the same bandwidth.

$$∀j=1\ldots n\quad c_{jj}=\sqrt{a_{jj}-\sum_{k=\max{(1,j-b)}}^{j-1}\lvert c_{jk}\rvert^2} \quad\textrm{ and }\quad ∀i=j+1\ldots \min{(n,j+b)} \quad c_{ij}=\frac{a_{ij}-\sum_{k=\max{(1,j-b)}}^{j-1}c_{ik}\overline{c_{jk}}}{c_{jj}}$$


:::: {.columns}

::: {.column width="34%" .fragment .fade-up}

::: {#09fa9d64 .cell execution_count=7}
``` {.julia .cell-code code-fold="false" code-line-numbers="false"}
function cholesky_banded(A, b)
    n = size(A, 1)
    C = copy(LowerTriangular(A))
    for j ∈ 1:n
        m, M = max(1,j-b), min(n,j+b)
        Cⱼₖ = C[j, m:j-1]
        C[j,j] -= Cⱼₖ'Cⱼₖ
        C[j,j] = √C[j,j]
        for i ∈ j+1:M
            C[i,j] -= Cⱼₖ'C[i, m:j-1]
            C[i,j] = C[i,j]/C[j,j]
        end
    end
    return C
end
```
