## More general basic iterative methods {.smaller}

Consider the splitting
$$
\mat A = {\color{green}\mat M} - {\color{blue} \mat N}
$$
where $\mat M$ is invertible and easy to invert.
Then
$$
\mat A \vect x_* = \vect b \quad \Leftrightarrow \quad
{\color{green}\mat M} \vect x_* = {\color{blue} \mat N} \vect x_* + \vect b
$$
which suggests the iterative method
$$
{\color{green}\mat M} \vect x^{(k+1)} = {\color{blue} \mat N} \vect x^{(k)} + \vect b
$$

. . .

:::{.callout-tip}
## Standard basic iterative methods (here $\mat A = \mat L + \mat D + \mat U$)
- Richardson's iteration corresponds to the particular splitting

$$
\mat A = \underbrace{{\color{green}\frac{1}{\omega} \mat I }}_{\mat M} - \underbrace{\left({\color{blue} \frac{1}{\omega} \mat I - \mat A}\right)}_{\mat N}
$$

- Jacobi's iteration: $\mat A = {\color{green}\mat D} - ({\color{blue}-\mat L - \mat U})$.

- Gauss Seidel's iteration: $\mat A = ({\color{green}\mat D + \mat L}) - ({\color{blue}- \mat U})$

- Relaxation method: $\mat A = \left({\color{green}\frac{\mat D}{\omega} + \mat L }\right) - \left({\color{blue}\frac{1 - \omega}{\omega} \mat D - \mat U} \right)$.
  $~$(Coincides with Gauss-Seidel when $\omega = 1$)
:::

## Convergence of general basic iterative method {.smaller}

:::{.callout-note}
## Proposition: convergence of the splitting method
- The iteration
  $$
  {\color{green}\mat M} \vect x^{(k+1)} = {\color{blue} \mat N} \vect x^{(k)} + \vect b
  $$
  converges for any initial $\vect x^{(0)}$
  if and only if $\rho({\color{green}\mat M}^{-1} {\color{blue} \mat N}) < 1$.

- In addition, for any $\varepsilon > 0$ there exists $K > 0$ such that
  $$
      \forall k \geq K,
      \qquad
      \norm{\vect x^{(k)} - \vect x_*} \leq \bigl(\rho({\color{green}\mat M}^{-1} {\color{blue} \mat N}) + \varepsilon\bigr)^k \norm{\vect x^{(0)} - \vect x_*}.
  $$
:::

. . .

:::{.callout-tip}
## Proof
Subtracting the equations
$$
\begin{aligned}
{\color{green}\mat M} \vect x^{(k+1)} &= {\color{blue} \mat N} \vect x^{(k)} + \vect b \\
{\color{green}\mat M} \vect x_* &= {\color{blue} \mat N} \vect x_* + \vect b
\end{aligned}
$$
and rearranging gives
$$
\vect x^{(k+1)} - \vect x_*
= {\color{green}\mat M}^{-1} {\color{blue} \mat N} (\vect x^{(k)} - \vect x_*)
= \dots = ({\color{green}\mat M}^{-1} {\color{blue} \mat N})^{k+1} (\vect x^{(0)} - \vect x_*).
$$
The "only if" part of the first item is simple.
The other claims follow from Gelfand's formula:
$$
\forall \mat B \in \complex^{n \times n}, \qquad
\lim_{k \to \infty} \norm{\mat B^k}^{1/k} = \rho(\mat B).
$$
:::

## Concrete convergence guarantees {.smaller}

Settings where [$\rho(\mat M^{-1} \mat N) < 1$]{.red} are identified on a case by case basis:

- Richardson's iteration, [sufficient condition]{.green}: $\mat A$ symmetric positive definite;

- Jacobi's iteration, [sufficient condition]{.green}: $\mat A$ strictly row or column diagonally dominant:

$$
    \lvert a_{ii} \rvert > \sum_{j \neq i} \lvert a_{ij} \rvert \quad \forall i
    \qquad \text{ or } \qquad
    \lvert a_{jj} \rvert > \sum_{i \neq j} \lvert a_{ij} \rvert \quad \forall j.
$$

- Gauss Seidel's iteration, [sufficient condition]{.green}: $\mat A$ strictly row or column diagonally dominant;

- Relaxation method, [sufficient condition]{.green}: $\mat A$ symmetric positive definite and $\omega \in (0, 2)$;

- Relaxation method, [necessary condition]{.blue}: $\omega \in (0, 2)$;

- ...

## Improving upon Richardson's iteration {.smaller}

**We suppose again, until the end of this lecture, that $\mat A$ is [symmetric positive definite]{.red}**

We recall Richardson's iteration:
\begin{align*}
\vect x^{(k + 1)} = \vect x^{(k)} - \omega \nabla f(\vect x^{(k)}), \qquad f(\vect x) := \frac{1}{2} \vect x^\t \mat A \vect x - \vect b^\t \vect x.
\end{align*}

:::{.callout-note}
## Proposition
For any $\vect d \in \real^n$, it holds that
$$
\argmin_{\omega \in \real} f \left(\vect x - \omega \vect d \right) = \frac{\vect d^\t \vect r}{\vect d^\t \mat A \vect d},
\qquad \vect r = \mat A \vect x - \vect b.
$$
:::

We can improve upon Richardson's iteration by letting
$$
    \vect x^{(k + 1)} = \vect x^{(k)} - \omega_{\color{red}k} \nabla f(\vect x^{(k)}),
    \qquad \omega_{\color{red}k} := \frac{\vect d_k^\t \vect d_k}{\vect d_k^\t \mat A \vect d_k},
    \qquad \vect d_k := \mat A \vect x^{(k)} - \vect b.
$$

This is the [steepest descent]{.green} method with optimal step.

